      "writtenOn": "2020-06-05T22:40:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I like where this is going. What do you think about:\n\n\n// Does not call into TCPC, just caches\nvoid typec_select_src_current(int port, enum tcpc_rp_value rp); \n\n// Does not call into TCPC, just caches (same as yours)\nvoid typec_select_collision_rp(int port, enum tcpc_rp_value rp);\n\n// Does not call into TCPC, just caches\nvoid typec_select_pull(int port, Rd/Rp);\n\n// Actually calls into TCPC with correct Rp, and CC information\nvoid typec_update_cc(int port)\n{\n rp \u003d cached_current_rp;\n\n // call into pe to determine if we are in explict contract\n if (rev3.0 and pe_in_explict_contract()) {\n   rp \u003d cached_collision_rp;\n }\n\n if (tc_state \u003d\u003d SRC_READ or SNK_READY) {\n   // ensure only one CC lines is getting set (unless in DBG ACC mode)\n }\n\n if (rd) {\n   tcpm_set_rds(port, polarity, both/one CC line);\n } else { //rp\n   tcpm_set_rps(port, polarity, both/one CC line, rp-value-from-above);\n }\n}\n\nAnd we call the typec_update_cc function after we call all of the select function we want to change. We also might be able to drop the caching in the tcpci layer too.",
